    <h2>What the Canopy Does to a Gutter Here</h2>

    <p>The tree-lined streets in the old textile-mill neighborhoods — Dallas Mill, Merrimack, and the modest bungalow blocks of Terry Heights and Sherwood Park — sit under some of the heaviest hardwood canopy in the city. That canopy is beautiful and it is relentless. Water oak and willow oak shed small leaves and catkins that pass right through a quarter-inch screen. Sweetgum drops spiky balls that plug an outlet in one storm. Loblolly pine adds needles that knit into a mat nothing short of fine mesh will stop. A gutter under this load is not a passive trough — it's collecting thirty-odd pounds of wet debris on a single forty-foot run, and everything about the install has to account for that weight.</p>

    <p>Builder-standard 5-inch gutter with a 2x3 downspout is sized for an open lot in a subdivision, not a shaded eave in an older neighborhood. The moment a leaf pack forms at the outlet, a 2x3 chokes and the whole run backs up and sheets over the front lip — usually right onto the fascia and the front door landing. On these lots the fix is 6-inch K-style with 3x4 downspouts, which passes roughly twice the debris-laden water before it dams. It is a bigger, less dainty-looking gutter, and on a tree-heavy house it is the difference between a system that works and one you clean out every month.</p>

    <h2>Why Historic Fascia Changes the Whole Job</h2>

    <p>The part no online quote captures is the wood the gutter hangs on. On homes built before 1940, the fascia is frequently true one-inch old-growth pine — denser and harder than the SPF a modern crew is used to. A standard 1-1/4-inch hanger screw skates on it and backs out within a season, which is why so many older-Huntsville gutters end up pulling away from the house. The right move is a 2-inch stainless screw driven into sound wood, hidden hangers tightened to 16 inches on center instead of the usual 24 to carry the debris weight, and honest replacement of any fascia section that's gone soft. Skip that last step and you're screwing an expensive new gutter into rot.</p>

    <p>The other quiet killer is a missing gutter apron. That's the strip of flashing that bridges the roof edge into the back of the gutter. Leave it off — and cheap installs almost always do, because it's a separate material and an extra step — and water wicks off the shingle edge, runs behind the gutter, and rots the fascia from behind where you can't see it. On a historic-district home with dimensional crown fascia, that repair costs more than the gutters did. A crew that installs the apron first is a crew that has replaced fascia before and doesn't want to come back to do it again.</p>

    <h2>Guards, Pitch, and the Overshoot Problem</h2>

    <p>Guards are worth it here, but only the right kind. Stainless micro-mesh keeps out the oak leaf litter and the pine needles that defeat everything cheaper. What the guard companies won't tell you: over a steep historic roof — a 6/12 or 8/12 pitch is common on the older two-stories — water leaves the shingles with real speed, hits the mesh, and can skip clean over the gutter in a downpour unless the gutter is set about a half-inch lower at the front than a bare gutter would be. Pitch matters too: a quarter-inch of fall per ten feet toward the outlet, no more, or long runs on a stately old eave start to look crooked and short runs overshoot.</p>

    <p>Honest limitation, because it's the thing homeowners get sold against: no guard is truly maintenance-free under a mature oak. Even flawless micro-mesh needs a blow-off once a year, because the mesh itself eventually films over with the fine yellow pollen this valley is famous for every spring. A gutter guard turns a four-times-a-year chore into a once-a-year one. It does not turn it into never.</p>

    <h2>Gutters Don't Live Alone on the House</h2>

    <p>Gutter work almost never shows up by itself. The same spring hailstorm that dents a roof bends the gutters, so a homeowner replacing both at once is smart to use one crew — most established <a href="/locations/huntsville/roofer/">Huntsville roofers</a> handle the gutter run in the same trip and flash it properly into the new drip edge. The black tiger-striping down the face of an older gutter isn't dirt you can hose off; it's oxidation and organic film that a <a href="/locations/huntsville/pressure-washing/">Huntsville pressure washing</a> crew soft-washes without denting the aluminum. And on the historic streets, gutter and fascia color has to match the trim, which is why the <a href="/locations/huntsville/painter/">painter</a> and the gutter installer should be talking before either orders material. If you want the full picture of contractor work across the city, the <a href="/locations/huntsville/">Huntsville contractor overview</a> lays it out trade by trade.</p>

    <h2 id="pricing-summary">What Gutters Cost in Huntsville</h2>

    <p>Real ranges, installed:</p>
    <ul class="trades-list-block">
      <li><strong>5-inch seamless aluminum</strong> — about $7 to $9 per linear foot.</li>
      <li><strong>6-inch seamless aluminum</strong> — about $10 to $13 per linear foot (the right call under heavy canopy).</li>
      <li><strong>Stainless micro-mesh guard</strong> — adds $9 to $16 per linear foot.</li>
      <li><strong>Fascia repair</strong> — priced separately, because on older homes you don't know what you've got until the old gutter is off.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>A tree-lined two-story with roughly 220 feet of run generally lands between $2,600 and $4,200. The spread is almost entirely about fascia condition and whether you guard the runs.</p>

    <h2>When to Do It</h2>

    <p>Late winter and early spring, before the pollen and the growing season, is the smart window — you want the new system in before the first heavy storms and before the sweetgum starts dropping. If your gutters overflowed last fall, don't wait for them to do it again; the water that sheets behind an overflowing gutter is what rots the fascia and, eventually, finds the crawlspace.</p>

    <h2>Questions Huntsville Homeowners Ask</h2>

    <p><strong>What size gutters do I need for a tree-shaded home?</strong></p>
    <p>6-inch K-style with 3x4 downspouts. The builder-standard 5-inch with 2x3 chokes the first time a leaf pack forms at the outlet, and under this canopy that's often.</p>

    <p><strong>Do gutter guards actually work here?</strong></p>
    <p>Stainless micro-mesh does. Screen and foam don't — needles and catkins get through or sit on top. Plan on one blow-off a year even with good mesh.</p>

    <p><strong>Why do my gutters pull away from the fascia?</strong></p>
    <p>Hangers driven into soft or old-growth fascia with screws too short to bite, or a missing gutter apron rotting the fascia from behind. Both are install shortcuts, not gutter defects.</p>

    <p><strong>What does it cost?</strong></p>
    <p>Roughly $7 to $13 a foot depending on size, plus guards and any fascia work. A typical shaded two-story runs $2,600 to $4,200.</p>

